<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=glassblowing">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glassblowing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as glass blowing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glassblowing is the technique of inflating molten glass into a bubble with a blowpipe and shaping it, developed by Phoenician and Syrian craftsmen in the 1st century BCE, in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and Israel. It quickly eclipsed casting and core-forming, spreading with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> to workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and beyond. Mould-blowing, introduced in the 1st century CE, allowed glass vessels to be made quickly in large quantities. Free-blowing remained the leading way of forming glass until the late 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glassblowing.jpg" alt="Glassblowing" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Le_ma%C3%AEtre_verrier_Werner_Garcia_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Glassblowing exploited a property of glass previously unknown to glassworkers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, the expansion of a molten blob of glass by introducing a little air. Molten glass is viscous enough to be blown and hardens gradually as it loses heat. A worker who blows glass is a glassblower, and the head of a workshop is a gaffer. The glass is gathered on the end of a blowpipe, rolled on a marver to form a cool skin, then inflated. The two main methods are free-blowing and mould-blowing. In free-blowing the worker blows short puffs of air into the gather and shapes it by rotating and swinging the pipe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Phoenician and Syrian craftsmen developed the technique in the 1st century BCE, and it quickly eclipsed casting and core-forming. Its invention coincided with the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, which helped it spread. The first large workshops stood in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and Israel and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where Ennion was among the most prominent glassworkers, known for complex multi-panelled mould-blown vessels. Mould-blowing, in which glass is inflated into a carved wooden or metal mould, came in the first part of the second quarter of the 1st century CE and allowed objects to be made quickly in large numbers.</p><p>Phoenician glassworkers set up workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by the middle of the 1st century CE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morgantina">Morgantina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, producing perfume containers, tableware and window glass. Craftsmen who had been forbidden to travel carried the technique north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Rhineland workshops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> made mould-blown flagons and bottles, and fragments of clay blowpipes survive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventicum">Avenches</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the tradition continued. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> made drinking vessels shaped like animal horns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> valleys, Byzantine workers made mould-blown glass with Christian and Jewish symbols in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and mould-blown vessels were found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>. Venetian glassworkers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murano">Murano</a> produced the fine glassware known as cristallo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and by the late 17th century blowing with the cylinder and crown methods made window panes. Free-blowing kept its leading position until the late 19th century and is still widely used for artistic purposes.</p><p>The studio glass movement began in 1962, when Harvey Littleton and Dominick Labino held two workshops at the Toledo Museum of Art, experimenting with a small furnace. Artists such as Dale Chihuly followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Glassblowing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
